  "rationale": "Suspected foreign body aspiration with air trapping is classically evaluated with inspiratory and expiratory chest X-rays; bronchoscopy follows if suspicion remains high.",
  "optionExplanations": {
    "A": "A lateral film may help localize some pathology but does not best confirm air trapping.",
    "B": "Bronchoscopy is definitive, but it is not the imaging step used to confirm air trapping.",
    "C": "CT is usually unnecessary in this setting and exposes the child to more radiation.",
    "D": "Decubitus views can sometimes help in young children, but inspiratory/expiratory films are the standard confirmatory radiographs.",
    "E": "Correct. Inspiratory and expiratory chest X-rays can demonstrate unilateral air trapping from an inhaled foreign body."
  },
